 How White Gold is tapping India’s gold resale market 
 Rahul Joseph founded White Gold in 2017 for gold resale.
 White Gold reached Rs 1,343 crore in revenue for FY26.
 The firm offers transparent pricing and instant payment.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
Rahul Joseph founded White Gold in 2017 to fill a gap in India’s gold resale market, where households had few organised alternatives to local jewellers for selling old jewellery. Nearly a decade later, the company reported revenue of Rs 1,343 crore in FY26, offering customers purity testing, transparent prices and immediate payment.
“At that point in India, there was no place anyone could go, get a value for their gold, sell it and get instant money,” Joseph said in an interaction with Moneycontrol. "The only option people had was to go back to the jeweller from whom they had bought the gold, and typically they would be told that the payment would take two or three days."
Joseph’s strategy was to make selling gold more transparent for customers who traditionally went to local jewellers. White Gold uses machines to test jewellery purity, displays the weight, and links its buying price to the market rate, so customers can see how the offer is calculated. It also pays customers immediately and provides a quote without melting or damaging their jewellery, so they can walk away if they are unhappy with the price.
Joseph built White Gold around three features he saw as missing from the traditional gold-selling process: transparent pricing, reliable purity testing and immediate payment. The company uses spectrometer machines to test purity and visible scales to weigh jewellery. It links its buying price to the MCX spot price through an API, updating the rate every few seconds after adding the company’s margin.
The idea was to reduce the subjectivity of a transaction that has historically depended heavily on the buyer's assessment of purity and value
When people buy jewellery, the experience is often associated with premium stores and a sense of occasion. Joseph wanted to extend that same sense of dignity to selling.
White Gold's stores have an open-plan layout without grills or barriers, and the company has deliberately positioned them as premium outlets, according to Joseph. Unlike jewellery stores, where buying and exchanging gold are the primary businesses and selling is often secondary,
White Gold's stores focus almost entirely on buying gold.
The transaction is designed to be quick. Joseph said a customer bringing in two or three pieces of jewellery can typically complete the process in about 30 minutes, including purity testing, weighing, know-your-customer checks, and payment. The company then transfers the money directly to the customer's bank account.
The company broke even in FY24 and has remained profitable since. Joseph says profits have grown sixfold since the company first became profitable, with an EBITDA margin of 3% to 4%
Revenue grew from Rs 70-75 crore in FY23 to Rs 238 crore in FY24, Rs 504 crore in FY25, and a provisional Rs 1,343 crore in FY26.
For FY27, the company is targeting Rs 3,000 crore in revenue. It already crossed Rs 1,200 crore at the time of the interaction. If achieved, the target would represent roughly 40 times its FY23 revenue.
The growth has come from both market expansion and a gradual shift in consumer behaviour, according to Joseph: more people are becoming comfortable selling gold through an organised player.
The expansion has been accompanied by a sharp increase in the volume of gold recycled. White Gold processed 1,218 kg in FY26 and expects to recycle 1,800-2,000 kg in FY27, Joseph said.
 What happens to the gold after it is sold? 
The consumer-facing transaction is only the first part of White Gold’s business model. Once a customer accepts the quote, the company buys the jewellery and transfers the money. From there, the gold enters a recycling chain. The gold is then processed and melted into bars, which are then certified. The company then sells these bars through an internal bidding system to buyers who quote for the gold. The highest bidder gets the bars, which are then sold into the jewellery trade.
This process could help reduce the need for imported gold if more recycled metal replaces fresh imports. The issue has gained attention amid the Prime Minister's recent appeal to reduce gold purchases, given India's dependence on imported gold. India held 34,600 tonnes of gold (as of June 2025) as per a Morgan Stanley report.
The model is different from the government’s Gold Monetisation Scheme. Rather than accepting gold as a deposit, White Gold buys the jewellery outright and pays the customer.
